  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
